<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taihō Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">701</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taihō Code was a Japanese legal and administrative code promulgated in 701. Modelled on the penal and administrative statutes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, it established a centralised bureaucratic state under the emperor. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty cite it as evidence of the influence of Tang institutions on neighbouring East Asian societies.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Taihō Code, in Japanese Taihō-ritsuryō, was compiled at the direction of Prince Osakabe, Fujiwara no Fuhito and Awata no Mahito, at the request of Emperor Monmu, at the end of the Asuka period. It was largely an adaptation of the governmental system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and is said to rest on the Code of Yonghui, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozong-of-tang">Emperor Gaozong of Tang</a> had implemented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 651. Its drafters were eighteen Japanese aristocrats and one Chinese scholar, Satsu Koukaku, who also took part in editing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a>. Its enactment was among the first events to give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> a significant place in Japanese ethics and government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The code set up two branches of government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Worship, the Jingi-kan, ranked higher and handled religious and ritual matters, including annual festivals, coronations and the upkeep of Shintō shrines, but had no connection with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The Department of State, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daijo-kan">Daijō-kan</a>, handled all secular business under a Great Council of State presided over by the Chancellor, with the Ministers of the Left and Right, four Great Councillors and three Minor Councillors, and eight ministries answerable to them.</p><p>The country was divided into provinces, kuni, with governors, kokushi, appointed by the central government, and into districts, gun, run by local officials who kept the peace, collected taxes, recruited labour for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> and kept registers of population and land allotment. There were sixty-six provinces and 592 districts when the code was enacted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The code departed from the Tang model in two main ways. Government positions and class status rested on birth, as in Japanese tradition, rather than on merit. And the Japanese rejected the Chinese idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, holding that the emperor's power came from his imperial descent and not from his righteousness as a ruler.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> period the code was revised to suit Japanese traditions and the practical needs of administration, and the revised version was called the Yōrō Code, whose major work was completed in 718. The Taihō Code survives only in an edition of 833, which contains the commentaries compiled in 718 and 833, so that it is hard to tell how much of the text is original.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taihō Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan's Taika Reform (645) and <b>Taihō Code</b> (701) explicitly modelled themselves on Tang institutions, and Japanese embassies to the Tang carried monks and students who brought Tang learning home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ise, dedicated to Amaterasu, was raised above all others and rebuilt for the first time in 690; the <b>Taihō Code</b> of 701 created a Council of Kami Affairs, the Jingikan, beside the Council of State; and the court commissioned the Kojiki in 712 and the Nihon Shoki in…</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Taiho code</b> of 701 gave Japan a full system of penal and administrative law modelled on Tang China, completing reforms begun under Yamato.</blockquote><a href="/state/ritsuryo-japan">Ritsuryo Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Taiho code</b> of 701 completed the transition to Ritsuryo Japan.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a></li></ul></section>
